<</AP<</N 870 0 R>>/BS 848 0 R/Contents(4.\tThe incompleteness of split lines must be made obvious. \r     totalSum = a + b + c +          \r                      d + e\);\r         function \(param1, param2,        \r                     param3\);\r          setText \("Long line split"        \r                            "into two parts."\);\r         for \(tableNo = 0; tableNo < maxTable;   \r                          tableNo += tableStep\) \rSplit lines occurs when a statement exceed the 80 column limit given above. It is difficult to give rigid rules for how lines should be split, but the examples above should give a general hint. \rIn general: \r   €Break after a comma. \r   €Break after an operator. \r   €Align the new line with the beginning of the expression on the                                                                                       previous line.\r)/CreationDate(D:20130628145017+05'00')/DA(0 G 0 g 0 Tc 0 Tw 100 Tz 16.3 TL 0 Ts 0 Tr /Cour 12 Tf)/DS(font: Cour 12.0pt;font-stretch:Normal; text-align:left; color:#000000 )/F 4/IT/FreeTextTypewriter/M(D:20130628145518+05'00')/NM(06953da5-a90d-411b-b3a5-81cd3a75931f)/P 844 0 R/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:10.0.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"  style="font-size:12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal;font-family:Cour;font-stretch:normal"><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">4.&#9;The incompleteness of split lines must be made obvious. &#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">     totalSum = a + b + c +          &#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">                      d + e\);&#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">         function \(param1, param2,        &#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">                     param3\);&#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">          setText \(&quot;Long line split&quot;        &#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">                            &quot;into two parts.&quot;\);&#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">         for \(tableNo = 0; tableNo &lt; maxTable;   &#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">                          tableNo += tableStep\) &#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">Split lines occurs when a statement exceed the 80 column limit given above. It is difficult to give rigid rules for how lines should be split, but the examples above should give a general hint. &#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">In general: &#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">   &#8226;Break after a comma. &#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">   &#8226;Break after an operator. &#13;</span></p><p dir="ltr"><span style="line-height:16.3pt;font-family:Courier">   &#8226;Align the new line with the beginning of the expression on the                                                                                       previous line.&#13;</span></p></body>)/Rect[82.473 404.335 608.075 712.469]/Subj(Typewritten Text)/Subtype/FreeText/T(adeelahmed)/Type/Annot>>
endobj
